WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.144
The lasting message of SPEED is that
parameterization deserves as much attention as the matching objective.

00:00:04.224 --> 00:00:08.368
By treating a synthetic dataset as a shared
dictionary of spatial-agnostic epitomic tokens,

00:00:08.448 --> 00:00:12.592
sparse per-image coding matrices,

00:00:12.672 --> 00:00:16.816
and a small recurrent network that reassembles them,

00:00:16.896 --> 00:00:21.040
SPEED removes the spatial redundancy that naive
methods leave on the table.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.264
The payoff is state-of-the-art distillation at a
fraction of the storage,

00:00:25.344 --> 00:00:29.488
biggest on high-resolution images,

00:00:29.568 --> 00:00:33.712
plus better generalization to unseen architectures
and stronger robustness to corruption.

00:00:33.792 --> 00:00:37.936
In short, sparse,

00:00:38.016 --> 00:00:42.160
shared representation is a powerful and general
lever for making tiny synthetic datasets do far more.
